# Environment contract for the `mandatex-ui` service (marketplace-ui).
#
# Copy values into Railway's service variables. Do not commit a filled-in copy.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS SERVICE HOLDS NOTHING.
#
# No signing key, no trust pins, no chain access, no database. It reads the
# marketplace API over HTTP and renders what comes back. That is the whole of it.
#
# Note what is absent below, and that the absences are different from each other:
#
#   MANDATEX_SIGNING_KEY*  — belongs to the verifier alone. This process scans its
#                            own environment at boot and exits 78 if it finds
#                            signing material, by variable name *and* by content,
#                            exactly as the app does.
#   MANDATEX_TRUST_*       — belongs to the app alone. The UI pins no keys and
#                            verifies no signatures; it displays verdicts the app
#                            already reached. Adding trust pins here would create a
#                            second, unreviewed place where "which signatures count"
#                            is decided.
#
# So the UI is outside the trust boundary in both directions, and the lockstep pair
# remains strictly verifier <-> app. That is why adding this service costs nothing in
# coordination: there is no third set of pins to keep in step.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The marketplace API this interface reads, over Railway's private network.
#
# Plaintext http is correct here and only here, for the same reason it is correct on
# the app's verifier hop: *.railway.internal is not reachable from outside the
# project and Railway's private network does not terminate TLS. The production https
# requirement is waived for the `.internal` suffix and nothing else — a loopback
# address such as http://127.0.0.1:8080 is refused with exit 78, which is why the UI
# is its own service rather than a second process inside the app's container.
MANDATEX_API_URL=http://mandatex-app.railway.internal:8080

# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

NODE_ENV=production

# Hard cap on request bodies, in bytes. The only body this service accepts is the
# mandate form post, so the default is already generous.
MANDATEX_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=1048576

# ── Set by the platform ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT — Railway injects this; the service binds it and falls back to 8081.
# HOST — defaults to 0.0.0.0, already set in the image.

# ── What the deployed UI will actually show ──────────────────────────────────
# Until the verifier has real agent artifacts, expect an honest but empty
# interface. `GET /v1/fixtures` on the app is unconditionally disabled under
# NODE_ENV=production and the vectors are not in any production image, so the UI
# renders its mandate form with a "no candidate attestations are available" banner
# and POST /evaluate answers 503.
#
# That is correct behaviour, not a broken deployment: this interface submits
# attestations issued by the verifier and cannot mint them. It becomes populated
# when the verifier can issue real ones. See "What is still missing" in README.md.
